  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...High pressure will continue to build across the\nMid-Atlantic and Carolinas today with a cooler/drier NE-E flow \nover the area bringing seasonable and comfortable temps for \nearly September, with highs in the low to mid 80s and dewpoints \ngenerally in the 60s. Temps will begin to warm mid to late week \nas the high shifts offshore allowing southerly flow to re- \ndevelop Wednesday onwards. High temps climb back into the upper\n80s to lower 90s and dewpoint temps into the low to mid 70s \nlate in the week. Mostly dry weather will prevail through mid \nweek as upper ridging build in from the west and a drier airmass\nin place, although as dewpoints and PWATs creep up late week,\nslight chance of showers have been introduced for the\nsea/sound/river breeze convergence zones (in the vicinity of \nMainland Dare/Hyde and East Carteret counties).\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Friday the high continues to shift offshore and\na front sweeps south through the Mid-Atlantic region. Ahead of\nthis front, continuous warm, moist advection in southerly flow \nwill help build PWATs to near 2\" by Friday. This will bring \nscattered to numerous sea breeze showers/tstorms back in the \nforecast for Friday. Meanwhile, despite the high shifting \noffshore, some residual ridging should slow down the progression\nof the front as it moves south Friday. Long range models are in\ngood agreement on this front stalling just to our north in VA, \nbut model trends will need to be analyzed in the coming days in \ncase the front shifts further south than what the 00Z guidance \nshows this morning. Along the front training \nshowers/thunderstorms are possible, and a narrow swath of deep \nlayer shear right along the front would bring both a low end \nhydro and severe threat. However, given the front is currently\nforecast to stall to our north, at this point both hydro and \nsevere potential is limited Friday. IF the front shifts further \nsouth into eastern NC, collisions with the afternoon sea breeze \ncould result in a more messy situation, but this is a worst case\nscenario that is not backed by most models at this time. \n\nThe front lifts back north Saturday into Sunday as a shortwave\nmoves through the region. This shortwave will bring slightly\nbetter chances for showers and thunderstorms this weekend. \nShear doesn't look to be impressive, but ample moisture will \npromote a marginal hydro threat. There is some uncertainty with\nhow much instability is in play Saturday, as it is dependent on\ncloud cover and associated surface heating. In a low-end CAPE \noutcome, (500-1000J/kg) we would have long skinny CAPEs that \nsuggest just a hydro threat and less frequent lightning. \nMeanwhile a high- end CAPE outcome (2000-4000 J/kg) would bring \nmore pulse- like thunderstorms capable of producing some gusty \nwinds, frequent lightning, and heavy rainfall rates. Regardless \nof how Saturday plays out, there is higher confidence on a low-\nCAPE outcome Sunday. While heavy showers and some thunderstorms \nare possible Sunday with the shortwave, Severe threat appears to\nbe more muted.",
